[# Section 17 — operations: running CX in production. Process model,
   the store service daemon, deployment artifacts, environment knobs,
   GC posture, logging — and, folded in here under DOCS-46 from the
   developer set that used to sit under docs/dev/, the daemon's
   clean-state bootstrap, its one authority table, observability and
   backpressure, and the XAP host. Sources: tooling/cxstore/, vcx/cmd
   dispatch, the store and XAP corpora ([fixture id=] citations replayed
   when the page is built) and the live binary — every command and
   output on this page was run before it landed here (issue #425
   coverage gap). #]

[section id=operations group='The platform group' n=17 title='Operations'

  [intro
    """CX in production is deliberately boring: a static binary, a
       foreground process, explicit capability grants (see
       [[capabilities]]), the host OS as the supervisor, and a small
       set of environment knobs. This section covers the process
       model, the store service daemon — its listeners, its clean-state
       bootstrap, its one authority table, its observability and
       backpressure — the XAP host, the deployment artifacts, the
       environment variables that matter, the collector at a glance,
       and logging. Every claim here stands beside the corpus case this
       guide's `cx` replayed when the page was built, or names the spec
       page that holds it."""]

  [child id=ops-process n=17.1 title='Process model'
    [body
      """Every CX process — a one-shot program, an HTTP service, the
         store daemon, a XAP host — runs in the **foreground** and owns
         its lifecycle. There is no built-in daemonizer and no bespoke
         supervision machinery, by explicit project decision:"""]
    [list
      [item """**The port is the only mutex.** A serving process that
              cannot bind fails fatally and loudly — it never
              half-runs. At most one instance per port, arbitrated by
              the kernel; replacing a running instance is an explicit
              stop-then-start, never a silent takeover."""]
      [item """**A crash is a bug to root-cause, not respawn over.**
              Development and attended operation run unsupervised.
              Unattended operation delegates restarts to the OS
              supervisor — systemd on Linux, launchd on macOS — which
              is also where restart policy, resource limits, and
              journaling belong."""]
      [item """**Clean shutdown on SIGTERM.** Long-running processes
              stop accepting, drain in-flight work, flush, and exit 0.
              `kill -TERM` is the normal stop verb everywhere."""]
      [item """**Bounded serving.** HTTP serving separates reactors
              (socket owners) from a bounded executor pool running
              handlers; overflow answers 503 rather than freezing the
              accept plane. Pool sizes are deploy-time environment
              config (subsection on environment variables below)."""]]
    [body
      """The two long-running processes the platform ships are the
         store daemon ([[ops-store-daemon]]) and the XAP host
         ([[ops-xap-host]]); both follow every rule above."""]]

  [child id=ops-store-daemon n=17.2 title='The store service daemon'
    [body
      """Two daemons ship with the platform. The first is the CX Store
         service: `cx store-serve` runs a multi-threaded
         content-addressed store server over the **XSP store profile**
         — the store wire, reached by clients as `cx-store://` (TLS)
         or `cx-store+xsp://` (cleartext dev) — with an optional gRPC
         edge listener; plain HTTP serves only the bootstrap endpoints
         (health, ready, metrics, capabilities). Its sibling,
         `cx fabric-serve`, runs the single-node fabric eventing tier:
         journal-backed fabrics served to XSP-AUTH clients over raw
         XSP frames, configured the same way (`--config
         fabric.service.cx`; the fabric spec is
         `spec/03-approved/platform/fabric.md`). The rest of this
         subsection covers the store daemon. It is configured by a CX
         document and gated by the ordinary capability flags — serving
         binds a socket, so it needs `--allow-net`:"""]
    [example lang=sh
      """$ cx store-serve --config /etc/cxstore/cxstore.service.cx --allow-net"""]
    [body """The config is itself CX data — this is a complete minimal
            config serving one in-memory store on loopback:"""]
    [example lang=cx
      """[cxstore-service
  [bind addr="127.0.0.1:18761"]
  [stores [store name="docs" url="mem://docs"]]]"""]
    [body
      """On startup the daemon states exactly what it is doing —
         including an honest warning when you have not configured
         auth:"""]
    [output
      """cx store-serve: listening on tcp://127.0.0.1:18761 — 1 store(s): docs (mem://docs), 4 workers, auth OPEN — no [xsp [grants …]] configured, anonymous full access (configure grants for production)"""]
    [body
      """Liveness and readiness are plain HTTP endpoints, and the
         binary doubles as its own probe (`cx store-health` exits 0
         iff the daemon reports accepting — this is what the Docker
         HEALTHCHECK and load balancers call):"""]
    [example lang=sh
      """$ curl -s http://127.0.0.1:18761/cx-store/v1/health
[health [status "ok"]]
$ curl -s http://127.0.0.1:18761/cx-store/v1/ready
[ready [accepting true] [draining false]]
$ cx store-health --url http://127.0.0.1:18761/cx-store/v1/ready && echo ready
ready"""]
    [body """Lifecycle, in signals:"""]
    [table
      [tr [th 'signal'] [th 'effect']]
      [tr [td 'SIGTERM / SIGINT'] [td 'stop accepting, drain in-flight requests, checkpoint every mounted store, exit 0']]
      [tr [td 'SIGHUP'] [td 'reload the hot config sections — tls, timeouts, limits, observability — without dropping connections, validate-then-swap; bind/stores/workers changes refuse the reload as restart-required. This is the signal an ExecReload= directive should send (the shipped unit does not carry one yet)']]]
    [body
      """**Two listeners.** `[bind addr=…]` is required and is a
         bootstrap-only HTTP surface: `health`, `ready` and
         `capabilities` under `/cx-store/v1/`, plus a daemon-level
         `/metrics`. It carries no store operation — any other path
         answers 404 `CXER1709` naming the profile as the wire. The
         data wire is the XSP store profile on its own listener
         (`[xsp enabled=true addr=…]`), with the optional gRPC edge
         (`[grpc enabled=true addr=…]`) beside it; the three addresses
         must differ, and `[xsp enabled=true]` requires an
         `[identity did= seed-env=]` — attach is XSP-AUTH and there is
         no anonymous responder. `capabilities` is the discovery advert:
         `wire-version`, `server-impl`, `config-generation`, the
         encodings, the `admin-ops` this daemon routes, the request and
         response size caps, the rate limit and — when the profile
         listener is up — `[xsp [addr …] [did …]]`, so a management
         client learns the store wire's address and responder DID from
         the bootstrap port instead of being configured with them
         twice."""]
    [body
      """Two operator verbs round out the surface. `cx store-rotate-kek`
         re-wraps an encrypted store under a new key-encryption key,
         resumable and fail-closed ([[store-security]]). `cx
         store-mint-principal` mints an XSP-AUTH principal offline —
         the next subsection walks a deployment from a clean state with
         it. There is no token-minting verb, because authority is not a
         bearer secret: clients hold DIDs, and the daemon's
         `[xsp [grants …]]` table decides every operation
         ([[ops-authority]]). Metrics, tracing, logs and backpressure
         are [[ops-observability]]; the gRPC edge is
         [[store-transports]]."""]]

  [child id=ops-bootstrap n=17.3 title='Clean-state bootstrap — mint, grant, serve, present'
    [body
      """Store credentials are **XSP-AUTH principals**: an Ed25519
         `did:key` whose seed the holder proves possession of per
         attach. `[xsp [grants …]]` is the only grant table, and a
         non-empty table means deny-by-default — the daemon admits
         exactly the DIDs written into its config and refuses every
         other principal. That leaves one question a fresh deployment
         has to answer: where does the *first* principal come from?
         `cx store-mint-principal` answers it offline — it generates
         the seed and derives the DID locally, touching no network and
         no store. Minting grants nothing; the config is still the sole
         authority."""]
    [body
      """**1 — Mint the principal.** The seed lands in a `0600` file and
         is never printed. `--caps` is required — the authority a grant
         carries is stated at mint time, never inherited from a
         default:"""]
    [example lang=sh
      """$ cx store-mint-principal --id ops --seed-file ./ops.seed --caps "read write admin\""""]
    [body
      """stdout is one canonical CX element — machine-readable and
         copy-paste-able — carrying the DID, the config stanza to
         splice into the daemon's `[xsp …]` section, and the open-opts
         a client presents:"""]
    [output
      """[xsp-principal id=ops for=grant did=did:key:z6Mk… seed-file='./ops.seed' seed-env=CX_XSP_SEED_OPS
  [config-stanza [xsp [grants [grant did=did:key:z6Mk… caps='read write admin']]]]
  [client-opts [map xsp-did=did:key:z6Mk… xsp-seed-env=CX_XSP_SEED_OPS]]]"""]
    [body
      """Operator guidance goes to stderr. Back the seed file up — it is
         the only proof of that DID, and a lost seed means re-minting
         and re-granting. Re-running the verb against an existing
         `--seed-file` is refused; `--force` replaces it and
         invalidates the old DID. `--id` must be the canonical spelling
         of the environment variable it derives — lowercase, `-` for
         word breaks: `CX_XSP_SEED_<NAME>` upper-cases and folds `-` to
         `_`, so `fleet_ops` and `Fleet-Ops` would derive the same
         variable as `fleet-ops`, and two principals minted under two
         spellings would silently share one seed. The verb refuses the
         non-canonical spellings, naming the id to use and the variable
         they would have shared."""]
    [body
      """**2 — Mint the daemon's own identity.** Attach is XSP-AUTH in
         both directions, so the daemon needs a responder identity of
         its own. Same verb, `--for identity`, and no `--caps`: the
         daemon's identity is the root of its authority, not a
         grantee."""]
    [example lang=sh
      """$ cx store-mint-principal --id host --seed-file ./host.seed --for identity"""]
    [body
      """**3 — Assemble the config.** Both `[config-stanza]` bodies
         splice into the same `[xsp …]` section verbatim — the identity
         row from step 2, the grant row from step 1 (the fabric daemon
         takes the same `[identity did= seed-env=]` row at its own top
         level):"""]
    [example lang=cx
      """[cxstore-service
  [bind addr="127.0.0.1:18761"]
  [stores [store name="main" url="file:///var/lib/cxstore/main"]]
  [xsp enabled=true addr="127.0.0.1:18762"
    [identity did="did:key:z6MkHost…" seed-env="CX_XSP_SEED_HOST"]
    [grants [grant did="did:key:z6Mk…" caps="read write admin"]]]]"""]
    [body
      """**4 — Serve.** The daemon resolves `seed-env` while parsing the
         config, so the host seed is exported *before* it starts. With
         grants present the daemon is deny-by-default from the first
         attach; no separate switch turns enforcement on:"""]
    [example lang=sh
      """$ export CX_XSP_SEED_HOST="$(cat ./host.seed)"
$ cx store-serve --config cxstore.service.cx \\
    --allow-read --allow-write --allow-env \\
    --allow-net=127.0.0.1:18761 --allow-net=127.0.0.1:18762"""]
    [body
      """The startup line says which posture it came up in — `auth
         ENFORCED (XSP-AUTH, N grant(s))` with a grant table, `auth OPEN
         — no [xsp [grants …]] configured, anonymous full access`
         without one. Read it on every deploy; it is the daemon's own
         answer, not an inference from the config file you think you
         shipped."""]
    [body
      """**5 — Present the identity.** The client loads the seed from
         the environment — never from the URL, never from an opts
         literal — and passes the `[client-opts]` map verbatim:"""]
    [example lang=sh
      """$ export CX_XSP_SEED_OPS="$(cat ./ops.seed)\""""]
    [; check=none — a live daemon on the loopback port is the program's
       input; no corpus case owns the presented-identity open. ]
    [example lang=cx check=none
      """[?lib 'cx-platform/store' :as store]
[?let [= $c [$store:open-opts 'cx-store+xsp://127.0.0.1:18762/main/'
              [map xsp-did='did:key:z6Mk…' xsp-seed-env='CX_XSP_SEED_OPS']]]
  [$store:put-doc $c [note [body 'first write']]]]"""]
    [body
      """A principal that was never granted is an equally well-formed
         identity and is still refused: the daemon rejects the op and
         the client raises `CXER1131` (`E_STORE_AUTH_FAILED`) naming the
         rejected wire op. Deny-by-default is a property of the config,
         not of the credential's cryptographic quality."""]]

  [child id=ops-authority n=17.4 title='Authority — one grant table'
    [body
      """There is exactly one authority model, shared by the profile
         listener and the gRPC edge with no second implementation.
         `[xsp [grants …]]` is the only grant table; the retired
         store-HTTP-era `[auth …]` section — static tokens, JWT, OIDC,
         role bundles, the Bearer base — is a hard config error that
         names the replacement, never a silently ignored stanza. Each
         `[grant did="…" caps="…" over=?]` row compiles to an ordinary
         delegation in the session's authority basis at attach, so one
         decision function governs every verb — attenuation, revocation
         and explain come for free rather than as a parallel role
         engine:"""]
    [fixture id=store-grants-001-row-compiles-to-one-root-delegation]
    [list
      [item """**The capability grammar** is `read`, `write`, `delete`,
              `admin`, `peer`. An unrecognised class is a startup error
              listing the accepted set. `over="/path"` slices a grant to
              a subtree; `[grant floor=true caps="read"]` is the
              anonymous floor, paired with
              `[xsp [policy mode=floor floor=NAME]]`. `admin` carries
              `status`, `gc`, `mounts` and `config-reload`; `peer` is
              deliberately narrower than `read` — a peer daemon receives
              revocations without holding read on any data plane."""]
      [item """**Posture follows the table's presence.** Grants
              configured means deny-by-default, every verb checked at
              the policy enforcement point and the PEP's `[deny …]`
              value crossing the wire verbatim. No grants means the open
              dev posture: data verbs open, the daemon-level admin verbs
              still requiring a DID-proven principal. Absent is open,
              present is enforced; there is no third switch."""]
      [item """**Delegation and presentation.** A client may attenuate
              its authority further by presenting a credential chain
              (`[vp …]` on attach, or a later `phase=present`) whose
              terminal subject is the session principal byte for byte.
              Escalation on any axis refuses; a root issuer this
              deployment does not recognise compiles to nothing rather
              than conjuring authority; a credential from a revoked
              chain stops verifying at its next use."""]
      [item """**Revoking a principal** is a `[xsp [grants …]]` edit
              plus `SIGHUP`, and it is live: the reload re-folds the
              grant table into every session already attached, so the
              revoked principal is refused at its very next call, not at
              the next restart. Revocation reaches delegated authority
              too — a credential that principal presented only ever
              conveyed authority through the config grant it was rooted
              in. The gRPC edge compiles its basis per call and picks the
              change up the same way. A live session's authority only
              ever narrows on reload: remove every grant and the attached
              sessions keep enforcing until they re-attach."""]
      [item """**Client identity never rides the wire URL or a
              literal.** `xsp-did` names the DID, `xsp-seed-env` names
              the environment variable holding the seed."""]]
    [body
      """The grammar and the refusal codes are the XSP store profile's
         listener-model section (`spec/03-approved/xap/xsp_store_profile.md`)
         and the gRPC edge's `spec/03-approved/misc/cxstore-grpc.md` §4,
         which share the one calculus; the gRPC edge authenticates per
         call — the `authorization` metadata carries a `CxCall`
         credential signed by the presenting DID over the exact method
         path, the exact request bytes, a timestamp inside a freshness
         window and a single-use nonce — against the same table and the
         same PEP."""]]

  [child id=ops-observability n=17.5 title='Observability and backpressure'
    [list
      [item """**`GET /metrics`** — Prometheus exposition on the
              bootstrap port, an unauthenticated operator plane: the
              bind address is the operator's control, and a standard
              scraper cannot sign a request. It is not exempt from
              admission control — the global pre-auth bucket covers it,
              so a scrape flood cannot force unbounded work. Cardinality
              is bounded by construction (labels: endpoint, status,
              store — never per hash): request counters, histograms and
              bytes, an in-flight gauge, per-store document, object and
              dedup gauges. No fabricated zeros — a store-internal series
              attaches only when the backend instruments it."""]
      [item """**OpenTelemetry tracing** — off by default; W3C
              trace-context is propagated either way so logs correlate;
              OTLP/HTTP export when enabled."""]
      [item """**Structured logs** — one record per bootstrap-surface
              request (endpoint, store, status, latency, bytes in and
              out, trace id), CX or JSON, with the health and ready
              probes suppressed as noise. The record carries no
              credential material, and the startup banner redacts the
              userinfo of any mount URL that has one (an
              `ftp://user:pass@…` substrate) rather than printing
              it."""]
      [item """**Backpressure, on by default.** The bootstrap surface
              and the gRPC edge run a token-bucket rate limit plus a
              concurrency cap keyed on the resolved store name —
              tenant-level backpressure — behind a global pre-auth bucket
              covering unauthenticated traffic. Every admission rejection
              is `429 CXER1706` with `Retry-After`; draining or not ready
              is `503 CXER1708`. The profile listener carries its own
              flow control — `[xsp [limits pending-window= liveness-ms=
              handshake-ms= max-frame-bytes=]]` — and
              `[xsp [limits [pushdown steps= memory-mb=]]]` budgets
              daemon-side evaluation for the compute-class verbs: an
              exhausted budget is a loud typed refusal, never a daemon
              takedown."""]
      [item """**Reload, hot and restart-required.** `SIGHUP` (or the
              admin-gated `config-reload` op) is validate-then-swap,
              all-or-nothing, fail-closed. Hot: `[tls]` (the cert and key
              *contents*, so rotation needs no restart), `[timeouts]`,
              `[limits]`, `[observability]` and `[xsp [grants …]]`.
              Restart-required: `[bind]`, `[grpc]`, `[stores]`,
              `[workers]` and every part of `[xsp]` other than `[grants]`
              — a change there refuses the whole reload with `CXER1712`
              naming the offenders. Env-injected secrets are
              rotation-blind; use file-based sources to rotate."""]]
    [body
      """The service-tier contract is
         `spec/03-approved/misc/cxstore_service_tier_phase2.md`
         (config appendix, tenancy, observability); the tenant boundary
         it fixes — store-per-tenant, one dedup pool per mount — is
         taught in [[store-security]]. Intra-store tenancy, quotas and
         multi-node are the demand-gated next phase and are not
         implemented."""]]

  [child id=ops-xap-host n=17.6 title='The XAP host'
    [body
      """A XAP is authored as three data documents — a feature per
         grammar, the deployment document, a surface per medium
         ([[dist-authoring]]) — and served by one call:
         `[$xap:host XAP-DOC OPTS]` boots a complete XAP from its
         deployment document. It acquires the pinned packages, verifies
         them, composes the grammar (the compose gate at load),
         translates governance into runtime dials, loads each feature's
         contract module, and serves the standard surface —
         `GET /grammar`, `/features`, `/surface/<f>`, `POST /intent`,
         `GET /stream`. A XAP with no custom transport is zero server
         code; deployment-specific pieces (extra routes, ingest
         workers, a simulation cadence) register as adapters through
         OPTS and never fork the host. The reference deployment and
         its client are the XAP repository's `reference/shop` and
         `reference/shop-web-client` (their page: [[dist-reference-surfaces]]);
         a project built on them runs with three make targets:"""]
    [example lang=sh
      """$ make run     # the XAP on :9001, foreground (its ingest adapter by default)
$ make dev     # the XAP, the web client and a browser; Ctrl-C stops both
$ make check   # the drift gate — specs against the implementation, fails on drift"""]
    [body
      """The host obeys the process model above to the letter. The
         deployment entry uses `[$xap:host …]!` — the postfix that turns
         an error value into a fatal exit — so a XAP that cannot serve
         never half-runs its ingest workers; and the serve verb refuses
         a URL with no port, and refuses to serve an errored runtime
         at all, propagating the error rather than relabelling it:"""]
    [fixture id=xap-serve-004-portless-url-refused]
    [fixture id=xap-serve-009-an-err-runtime-does-not-serve]
    [body
      """Two layers of authority, both deny-by-default, and never
         blurred: **host capabilities** — `cx` grants `read`, `write`,
         `net`, `env` per invocation, and an ungranted effect raises
         `CXER0271` naming the flag ([[capabilities]]) — and **XAP
         authority** (the trust-model part of the XAP spec): authority
         originates only from principals, every intent passes the one
         policy enforcement point, grants are per verb, scoped and
         conditioned, and the dial moves *who drives*, never *what is
         permitted*. Identity is DID-first — `did:key`, offline, right
         for a disconnected deployment — and a delegation is a
         verifiable credential."""]
    [body
      """A session is a `(principal, tenant)` **attachment**: the server
         owns session state, clients attach, detach and mirror-attach,
         and the session survives the death of a client. Authoritative
         state is the journal fold, never the session. What runs today
         is the single in-process runtime a long-lived deployment uses;
         the multi-tenant gateway and worker topology is design-frozen
         and implemented incrementally (the session module spec and the
         XAP architecture notes). The attachment, replayed:"""]
    [fixture id=session-001-attach-binds-principal-tenant]
    [fixture id=session-011-mirror-attach-adds-client]
    [fixture id=session-014-detach-client-survives-zero]]

  [child id=ops-artifacts n=17.7 title='Deployment artifacts'
    [body
      """Reference deployment artifacts live in `tooling/cxstore/` in
         the repository — use them as the template rather than
         hand-rolling:"""]
    [list
      [item """**systemd unit** (`cxstore.service`) — `Type=notify`:
              the daemon signals `READY=1` only after the store is
              open and the listener is bound, so ordering and
              readiness are real, and it answers `WatchdogSec` pings.
              Install the unit, place the config at
              `/etc/cxstore/cxstore.service.cx`, then
              `systemctl enable --now cxstore`."""]
      [item """**Dockerfile** — a minimal `debian:stable-slim` image:
              copy in the static `cx` binary and the config; the
              image HEALTHCHECK runs `cx store-health` against
              `/cx-store/v1/ready`. One binary, one config file — the
              whole deployment surface."""]
      [item """**Sample config** (`cxstore.service.cx`) and a README
              with a copy-paste loopback smoke test."""]]
    [body
      """Building the artifact itself: `make build-vcx` produces the
         optimized binary at `vcx/target/cx`, and `make install-cli`
         installs it to the prefix. There is nothing else to ship — a
         CX deployment is the binary, your `.cx` files, and the
         capability flags in the unit file. The binary self-describes:
         `cx --version` reports version, build profile, commit, build
         date, the compiled collector, the V-fork pin, the compiled
         storage engines, and the feature set."""]]

  [child id=ops-env n=17.8 title='Environment variables'
    [body
      """The honest current set of deploy-relevant knobs, from the
         engine source. All are optional; the defaults are the
         recommendation unless you have measured otherwise:"""]
    [table
      [tr [th 'variable'] [th 'default'] [th 'meaning']]
      [tr [td 'CX_HTTP_N'] [td 'min(4, cores)'] [td 'HTTP reactor (socket-owner) count. An integer is honored as-is; `max` means one per core. Fan-out past 4 rarely helps — the default scales from laptop to server.']]
      [tr [td 'CX_HTTP_EXEC'] [td '16'] [td 'HTTP executor threads running handlers — deliberately above the reactor count so a handler parked on an upstream call never freezes the serve plane.']]
      [tr [td 'CX_HTTP_QUEUE'] [td '1024'] [td 'Pending-job queue depth; overflow answers 503.']]
      [tr [td 'CX_WORKER_THREADS'] [td '(unset)'] [td 'Sizes the worker thread pool — sizing only, never a semantics dial. [?worker] and [?async] bodies always spawn concurrently; the old =0 synchronous-fallback semantics are retired and non-conforming.']]
      [tr [td 'CX_REGISTRY'] [td '(unset)'] [td 'Store URL binding the feature registry for `pkg:` module references; unset, a `pkg:` resolution fails closed (CXER4889).']]
      [tr [td 'CX_STORE_KEK_<id>'] [td '(unset)'] [td 'Key-encryption-key material for encrypted stores, injected by id — key material never lives in config files.']]
      [tr [td 'VGC_MEMLIMIT_MB'] [td '2048'] [td 'Soft heap limit: the GC pacer is clamped here so collection engages well before the hard ceiling (the GOMEMLIMIT analog). Multi-GB deployments raise it deliberately.']]
      [tr [td 'VGC_MAX_ARENAS'] [td 'RAM-derived'] [td 'Hard heap ceiling in 64 MB arenas. The default sits where further growth would be swap death; hitting it dies loudly with forensics instead of a much-later OS kill. Override in either direction — lower to fence a process in, raise past RAM for deliberate overcommit.']]
      [tr [td 'VGC_GCTRACE'] [td '0'] [td 'Set to 1 for a per-collection trace line on stderr — permanent observability, one integer test per cycle when off.']]
      [tr [td 'VGC_HEADROOM_MB'] [td '64'] [td 'Ceiling for the adaptive pacer headroom. Rarely needed; measured throughput-equivalent well below the old thread-scaled values.']]
      [tr [td 'VGC_NEXT_GC_MB'] [td '(adaptive)'] [td 'Pins the pacer headroom to a fixed size — a testing/diagnostics knob (soundness gates run it at 4 to force frequent collections).']]]
    [body """`VGC_GCTRACE=1` output against an allocation-heavy
            program, verified live:"""]
    [output
      """[gc 0] marked=31MB goal=62MB headroom=16384KB pause=5660us arenas=1 spans=1477 pool=0KB trimmed=0KB threads=1
[gc 1] marked=62MB goal=125MB headroom=32768KB pause=25188us arenas=2 spans=5509 pool=4680KB trimmed=0KB threads=1
[gc 2] marked=123MB goal=246MB headroom=65536KB pause=64069us arenas=3 spans=13070 pool=0KB trimmed=0KB threads=1"""]]

  [child id=ops-gc n=17.9 title='Garbage collection at a glance'
    [body
      """The collector is a build-time property of the binary, and the
         binary tells you which one it carries:"""]
    [output
      """$ cx --version
cx vX.Y.Z             [# a RELEASE build — see below #]
  profile  platform
  commit   3ed9ade5
  built    2026-08-20T05:52:49Z
  gc       e — Perceus RC front line + precise STW vgc backstop
  V fork   b3d0da670
  engines  sqlite redis
  features (none)"""]
    [body
      """The headline is a **provenance claim**, not an echo of a version
         file. A binary reports the bare `cx vX.Y.Z` only when it was
         built from a clean checkout of the annotated release tag; every
         other build — yours, a CI build, a build from an edited tree —
         reports a pre-release of that version, with the build commit as
         metadata:"""]
    [output
      """$ cx --version
cx vX.Y.Z-dev+3ed9ade5
  profile  platform
  commit   3ed9ade5
  ..."""]
    [body
      """`-dev+<commit>` orders **before** the release it will become, so
         a version comparison against a shipped release answers correctly,
         and a work-in-progress binary can never be mistaken for the
         release whose number it carries. An edited tree marks both
         lines: `cx vX.Y.Z-dev+3ed9ade5-dirty`. What decides is git state
         at build time — the release version itself still comes from the
         one source of truth, the repo's `VERSION` file."""]
    [body
      """Mode `e` — the shipped default — is a two-layer design:
         precise reference counting (Perceus) reclaims most values
         immediately as the front line, and a precise stop-the-world
         tracing collector (vgc) backstops cycles and shared
         structures. Operationally that means: allocation-heavy
         programs pace themselves adaptively (collections start small
         and early, growing toward the soft limit), long-running
         services hold a steady RSS rather than ratcheting, and an
         out-of-memory at the hard arena ceiling is **loud** — a
         diagnostic dump at the wall, not a silent OOM-kill later.
         The three knobs that matter in production are
         `VGC_MEMLIMIT_MB` (pacing target), `VGC_MAX_ARENAS` (hard
         wall), and `VGC_GCTRACE` (observability) — see the table
         above. There is nothing to tune on day one."""]]

  [child id=ops-logging n=17.10 title='Logging and journaling'
    [body
      """Structured logging is a bundled module, not an external
         dependency. Leveled emits write to the active sink (stderr
         by default) and **return null** — bound and discarded here,
         so the nulls do not echo on stdout; `[$log:configure]` sets
         the minimum level, sink, and format process-wide:"""]
    [example lang=cx
      """[?lib 'cx-stdlib/log']
[?let [= $c [$log:configure {level: :warn}]]
      [= $i [$log:info 'not shown']]
      [= $w [$log:warn 'disk nearly full']]
  'logged']"""]
    [output
      """warn disk nearly full
'logged'"""]
    [body
      """Sinks include stderr, stdout, file (with size/time rotation),
         and syslog; formats include text, JSON-lines, and logfmt,
         with multi-sink fan-out — the full configuration surface is
         on the `log` module page in the Standard library section of
         this guide and in the module spec. Under systemd, logging to
         stderr and letting journald own retention is the intended
         default posture."""]
    [body
      """For audit-grade history there is `journal` — an append-only,
         hash-chained, tenant-partitioned event log over a store
         backend, with deterministic fold-to-state, replay, dry-run,
         verification, and signed snapshots. It is the persistence
         spine of the XAP runtime and usable standalone; see the
         `journal` module page in the Standard library section. The
         store daemon additionally exposes service-side observability
         (request metrics, optional OTel tracing, JSON access logs) —
         configured in the service config ([[ops-observability]])."""]
    [note
      """The operator's reading order for the whole platform runs
         through this guide: the store's embedded tier and porcelain
         ([[store]]), this page for the daemon and the host, the
         registries and the distribution machinery
         ([[xap-distribution]]), and the three contracts you operate
         under ([[bitemporal]], [[consistency]], [[event-evolution]]).
         Where a capability is designed but not yet shipped, the page
         says so by name; this section only teaches what the shipped
         binary was observed to do."""]]

  [child id=ops-sso-compat n=17.11 title='Identity-provider compatibility'
    [body
      """The question an enterprise evaluation actually asks is whether
         CX will just work against the identity provider that
         organization already runs, and the only honest answer is a
         table saying what was run, against what, and when. This is
         that table. It is kept honest by two lanes, and
         the split between them is the reason the table can be trusted:
         `make test-sso-interop-lane` runs on every commit against an
         identity provider that ships with the sso module itself. Since
         the module was extracted into its own repository that provider
         reaches a `cx` build as the pinned checkout `deps.cxd` names
         (`deps/cx-platform-sso/scripts/sso_interop/idp.cx`, after `make
         deps-sync`), and the lane still runs against the binary this
         tree built. `tools/sso_interop_real.sh PROVIDER`, in the same
         repository, runs against a real tenant at release time. A cell
         that names neither is an unverified cell, and it says so. A
         PARTIAL verdict names exactly which rows it rests on — in the
         OIDC and client-auth columns, not folded into the verdict word
         alone — so a partial run can never be read as more than what
         it drove."""]
    [body
      """What the per-commit lane grades: discovery and its issuer
         mix-up refusal, the authorization request as the library forms
         it, all four client-authentication methods at the token
         endpoint, PKCE verification failure, ID-token signature
         selection by `kid` across a two-key JWKS, refresh-token
         rotation, the machine grant, and the token endpoint's own
         OAuth error body surfacing to the caller. What it CANNOT
         grade: the interactive authorize leg. A real login puts a
         human at a sign-in page, so no unattended lane can perform one
         — the lane reads the `Location` the identity provider answers
         with and redeems the code from it, which is exactly what a
         callback handler does, but the consent screen in between is
         not exercised by anything here."""]
    [table
      [tr [th 'identity provider'] [th 'OIDC code + PKCE'] [th 'client auth (basic / post / private_key_jwt / none)'] [th 'SAML (assertion-signed / response-signed)'] [th 'SCIM'] [th 'verdict'] [th 'verified how'] [th 'verified at']]
      [tr [td 'Entra ID'] [td 'discover + JWKS VERIFIED 2026-09-11 (public tenant 9188040d-6c67-4c5b-b112-36a304b66dad, v2.0; 8 signing keys); auth-code leg PENDING a registered client'] [td 'PENDING (client_credentials / wrong-secret need a registered client)'] [td '--'] [td '--'] [td 'PARTIAL'] [td 'tools/sso_interop_real.sh entra (discovery mode)'] [td '2026-09-11']]
      [tr [td 'Okta'] [td '--'] [td '--'] [td '--'] [td '--'] [td 'NOT YET VERIFIED'] [td 'tools/sso_interop_real.sh okta -- pending a tenant'] [td '--']]
      [tr [td 'PingFederate / PingOne'] [td '--'] [td '--'] [td '--'] [td '--'] [td 'NOT YET VERIFIED'] [td 'tools/sso_interop_real.sh ping -- pending a tenant'] [td '--']]
      [tr [td 'ADFS'] [td '--'] [td '--'] [td '--'] [td '--'] [td 'NOT YET VERIFIED'] [td 'tools/sso_interop_real.sh adfs -- pending a tenant'] [td '--']]
      [tr [td 'Keycloak'] [td '--'] [td '--'] [td '--'] [td '--'] [td 'NOT YET VERIFIED'] [td 'tools/sso_interop_real.sh keycloak -- pending a tenant'] [td '--']]
      [tr [td 'cx reference IdP (deps/cx-platform-sso/scripts/sso_interop/idp.cx)'] [td 'yes -- code, state, nonce, S256 challenge, and the RFC 9207 iss parameter on the authorization response'] [td 'all four'] [td 'assertion-signed: fetched over http and attached, in plaintext and as an EncryptedAssertion (AES-256-CBC under RSA-OAEP-MGF1P) the relying party decrypts with its own private half; response-signed and the ACS POST binding are NOT graded'] [td 'not exercised -- this lane is the login half'] [td 'verified per commit -- test-sso-interop-lane'] [td 'rows: discover, mixup, login-none, login-secret-basic, login-secret-post, login-private-key-jwt, wrong-secret, pkce-mismatch, rotation, refresh, client-credentials, saml-over-wire, saml-encrypted-over-wire'] [td 'every commit']]]
    [note
      """Entra groups OVERAGE, recorded rather than left silent (RULED:
         1406-b). Above its threshold Entra stops emitting the `groups`
         claim and sends `_claim_names` / `_claim_sources` naming a
         Microsoft Graph endpoint instead. `session:map-claims` reads
         that and records `[groups-overage source=...]` with NO `groups`,
         and an `authz` decision over such a binding refuses with
         `:groups-indeterminate` -- never an empty allow. FETCHING the
         withheld set over Graph is the deployment's step and is
         DEFERRED: the trigger for building it is the first Entra tenant
         in this matrix over the overage threshold. Until then the
         fail-closed refusal is the whole behavior."""]
    [note
      """A `--` is "not measured", never "not supported". The five named
         products have no row content because no tenant has been driven
         yet, and `tools/release-verify.sh` refuses a release while any
         of Entra, Okta, Ping or ADFS still reads NOT YET VERIFIED --
         which is the point of writing the unverified cells down rather
         than leaving the table off the page."""]
    [note
      """Scheduled, and deliberately absent above rather than quietly
         implied: `client_secret_jwt` as a fifth client-authentication
         method (RULED 1409-a) and
         the opt-in acceptance of SHA-1 signatures that some long-lived
         ADFS deployments still emit (RULED 1410-a). The ACS POST
         binding and `RelayState` are owed to #1394 and #1402b. Until
         each lands, the matrix has no column for it."""]]
]
